  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>We know that the ideal gas equation of gas is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></math></p><p>Keeping the temperature constant, we have,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>P</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>As seen from graph,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>, so&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>.</p>"
